.TH GPSFS 4
.SH NAME
gpsfs \- GPS receiver as a file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpsfs
[
.B -m
.I mountpoint
] [
.I command ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Gpsfs
reads the NMEA 0183 sentences a GPS receiver sends, assembles them into
fixes with
.IR nmea (2),
and serves the receiver's position, and the traffic it hears, as
files, mounted by default at
.BR /mnt/gps .
A receiver is a byte stream: a serial port
.RI ( eia (3)),
a phone's own location service
.RI ( location (3)),
a network feed (a phone application serving NMEA over TCP,
.IR gpsd ,
a marine gateway's UDP), or a recorded log; and anything else writes
sentences to
.BR feed .
.PP
Besides NMEA, two formats are read: Garmin's Aviation output, from a
panel or portable aviation GPS
.RI ( garminav (2)),
and GDL 90, from an ADS-B receiver
.RI ( gdl90 (2)).
A fix from either is written as the NMEA sentences a receiver would
send for it, and read as those, so every file below means the same
whatever the receiver, and
.B nmea
gives NMEA from any of them.
The
.I command
words, if given, are written to
.B ctl
once the files are mounted.
.SH FILESYSTEM
.TF satellites
.TP
.B ctl
read: the source command in use, or nothing.
write: one command.
A source command returns once the source is open, or fails with the
reason it could not be opened; the previous source is stopped either
way.
.RS
.TP
.BI source " \fR[\fPformat\fR]\fP path \fR[\fPbaud\fR]"
a file or serial port, read to its end.
.I Format
is
.B nmea
(the default),
.B garmin
or
.BR gdl90 .
If
.I baud
is given,
.BI b baud
is written to
.IB path ctl
first, as
.IR eia (3)
expects.
.TP
.BI source " \fR[\fPformat\fR]\fP net\fB!\fPhost\fB!\fPport"
a network connection, as
.IR dial (2)
makes.
With host
.BR * ,
.BI udp!*! port
is listened to: the datagrams any sender sends to that port, as a
marine gateway (port 10110) or an ADS-B receiver (port 4000) sends
them; an NMEA datagram is whole, a newline or not.
.TP
.BI "source gpsd" " host"
.IR gpsd ,
at
.IR host ,
.IB host ! port ,
or a full dial address; port 2947 by default.
It is asked for NMEA with
.BR ?WATCH ;
its JSON lines are passed over.
.TP
.BI replay " path"
a recorded NMEA log, its sentences sent at the pace of their times (no
gap longer than five seconds).
.TP
.B stop
no source.
.RE
.TP
.B status
one line, attr=value:
.B state=fix
(the last fix is a position),
.B nofix
or
.BR nosource ;
.B link=none
.RB ( opening ,
.BR open ,
.B closed
when the source ended,
.BR error );
.BI source= address;
.BI sentences= n
and
.BI bad= n
counted since start;
.BI age= seconds
since the last position;
.BI error= reason
for the last source that failed, quoted as
.IR tokenize
(see
.IR sys-tokenize (2))
expects.
.TP
.B position
.I "latitude longitude"
in decimal degrees, north and east positive, six places.
A read is an error,
.BR "no fix" ,
when the receiver has no position.
.TP
.B fix
the last fix, one line of attr=value fields, each given only when the
receiver sent it:
.BR time
(RFC 3339, UTC),
.BR lat ,
.BR lon ,
.B alt
(metres above mean sea level),
.B baroalt
(pressure altitude, metres: Garmin's PGRMZ or GDL 90's own-ship report),
.B speed
(metres a second over the ground),
.B course
(over the ground, degrees true),
.B mcourse
(the same, magnetic),
.B heading
(where the bow or nose points, degrees true, from a compass: HDT, HDG,
HDM),
.B mheading
(the same, magnetic),
.B mode
.RB ( none ,
.BR 2d ,
.BR 3d ),
.B q
(how it was found:
.BR gps ,
.BR dgps ,
.BR pps ,
.BR rtk ,
.BR float ,
.B dr
for dead reckoning,
.BR manual ,
.BR sim ),
.B sats
(satellites used),
.BR hdop ,
.BR vdop ,
.BR pdop ,
.B err
and
.B verr
(horizontal and vertical error estimates, metres, one sigma: GST, else
Garmin's PGRME),
.B magvar
(the magnetic variation, degrees, east positive).
Magnetic directions are given only when the receiver gives the
variation.
With no fix the line is the time and
.BR mode=none .
.TP
.B event
each read waits for the next fix and gives it, as
.B fix
would, or the fixes since the last read.
Every open has its own; a reader that falls far behind loses the
oldest.
A fix is given once the receiver has sent all it sends for one time,
or a sentence for the next time has come; a lost fix is told once.
The first fix from a new source is given at its first position
sentence, before the receiver is known to send others.
.TP
.B satellites
one line a satellite in view:
.I "system prn elevation azimuth snr used"
where
.I system
is
.BR gps ,
.BR sbas ,
.BR glonass ,
.BR galileo ,
.BR beidou ,
.BR qzss ,
.B navic
or
.BR gnss ;
.B -
stands for a value not known (an
.I snr
for a satellite not tracked), and
.I used
is 1 for a satellite in the fix.
.TP
.B traffic
one line a craft heard in the last ten minutes, nearest first, of
attr=value fields:
.B kind
.RB ( ais
for a ship,
.B adsb
or
.B flarm
for an aircraft),
.B id
(an MMSI, or an ICAO or FLARM address in hex),
.BR lat ,
.BR lon ,
.B alt
(metres; pressure altitude by ADS-B, above this fix's altitude by FLARM),
.BR speed ,
.BR course ,
.BR heading ,
.B climb
(metres a second),
.B name
(a ship's name, a flight's call sign),
.B callsign
(a ship's radio call sign),
.B cat
(a ship's type:
.BR cargo ,
.BR tanker ,
.BR passenger ,
.BR fishing ,
.BR sailing ,
.BR pleasure ,
.BR tug ,
\&...; an aircraft's:
.BR light ,
.BR large ,
.BR heavy ,
.BR rotorcraft ,
.BR glider ,
.BR uav ,
\&...) and
.B age
(seconds since last heard).
Ships come from AIS sentences (messages 1, 2, 3, 5, 18, 19 and 24;
those of more than one sentence put together), aircraft from GDL 90's
traffic reports and FLARM's PFLAA, which FLARM gives relative to this
position.
A name with blanks is quoted.
.TP
.B nmea
the sentences, as received (AIS's, beginning
.BR ! ,
too), one a line, from the open on; each read waits for more.
Sentences that fail their checksum are not passed on.
.TP
.B feed
write-only: sentences written here are taken as the receiver's.
A write with a sentence that is not one (no
.B $
or
.BR ! ,
a bad checksum) fails, naming the fault; the others in it are taken.
.SH CAPABILITY MODEL
Following the receiver and changing what it says are separate files,
so what a process may do is what is bound in its name space:
.BR position ,
.BR fix ,
.BR event ,
.BR satellites ,
.B traffic
and
.B nmea
let it follow the receiver;
.B ctl
and
.B feed
let it choose the source or supply a position.
A process that should only know where it is gets the first six.
.PP
.I Gpsfs
opens sources in the name space it was started in.
A serial port can always be named in the kernel's own syntax,
.BR #t/eia0 .
.SH EXAMPLES
On a phone:
.IP
.EX
; gpsfs source '#g/nmea'
.EE
.PP
A USB receiver on Linux, which
.IR eia (3)
finds after the
.B ttyS
ports:
.IP
.EX
; cat '#t/eia4status'
opens 0 ferr 0 oerr 0 baud 9600 ... dev /dev/ttyACM0
; gpsfs source '#t/eia4' 9600
; cat /mnt/gps/position
40.784000 -74.416667
.EE
.PP
A phone serving NMEA on port 11123, and a moving map's loop:
.IP
.EX
; echo source tcp!phone!11123 > /mnt/gps/ctl
; cat /mnt/gps/event
time=2026-10-31T14:32:05Z lat=40.784000 lon=-74.416667 alt=76.0 ...
.EE
.PP
A boat: a gateway on the boat's network sending NMEA 0183 (from its
NMEA 2000 bus) to port 10110, the position, heading and AIS ships in
one stream:
.IP
.EX
; echo source udp!*!10110 > /mnt/gps/ctl
; cat /mnt/gps/traffic
kind=ais id=211234560 lat=53.543200 lon=9.987600 speed=6.3 course=87.6 heading=88 name='MV EXAMPLE' callsign=DABC cat=cargo age=4.2
.EE
.PP
An aircraft: an ADS-B receiver (Stratux and the like) broadcasting GDL
90, and a panel GPS's Aviation output on a serial port:
.IP
.EX
; echo source gdl90 udp!*!4000 > /mnt/gps/ctl
; echo source garmin '#t/eia0' 9600 > /mnt/gps/ctl
.EE
.PP
A day's drive, again:
.IP
.EX
; cat /mnt/gps/nmea > /usr/$user/track.nmea &
\&...
; echo replay /usr/$user/track.nmea > /mnt/gps/ctl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/gpsfs.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/nmea.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/gdl90.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/garminav.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR nmea (2),
.IR gdl90 (2),
.IR garminav (2),
.IR eia (3),
.IR location (3),
.IR wm-road (1)
.SH BUGS
One source at a time.
A source that ends or fails is not reopened; write the command again.
.PP
Neither Garmin's Aviation output nor GDL 90 gives the date and time
with each fix, so gpsfs stamps them with this host's clock.
.PP
It is not navigation equipment: nothing here is certified, there is no
integrity monitoring (RAIM), and a phone's position may come from
Wi-Fi.
A pressure altitude is as the device gives it; it is not corrected
for the local altimeter setting.
